WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:15.119
"These are the times that try men's souls.
The summer soldier and the sunshine patriot

00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:22.420
will, in this crisis, shrink from the service
of his country. But he that stands it now

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:31.460
deserves the love and thanks of man and woman."
Thomas Paine, "The American Crisis." December

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:37.629
19th, 1776.

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:45.420
America has had many crises since the winter
of 1776, though none so bleak as that first

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:57.809
one. We're in a crisis now, caused by a falling
away from the principles of 1776.

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:02.190
The immediate way to understand the American
crisis is the fact that the government is

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:07.329
at the point of becoming an unlimited government.
I mean, goodness, it's about to bankrupt the

00:01:07.329 --> 00:01:09.369
richest country in the history of man.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:16.869
It is a crisis unlike anyone that is previously
met, because it is self-inflicted and it is

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:19.579
wholly avoidable.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:26.029
America has been in a slow-motion emergency
since Progressivism began to erode our Constitutional

00:01:26.029 --> 00:01:27.909
foundations.

00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:32.779
We are the last best hope of the world and
we're into, in my mind, a very, very unfortunate

00:01:32.779 --> 00:01:37.149
period of turning our back on first principles.

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:42.759
We have to fight for our liberty and if we
don't have a rebirth in learning and liberty,

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:44.420
we're going to lose it.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:51.189
Any political renewal in the United States
will start from, be based on, the two great

00:01:51.189 --> 00:01:57.240
documents, the Declaration and the Constitution,
which means it begins with the study of those.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:03.279
In the mind of the Founding Fathers, what
is the responsibility of government?

00:02:03.279 --> 00:02:09.849
Because our college has never forgotten those
principles, never abandoned that task, we're

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:23.170
going to help provide that spark.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.660
The Founding Fathers, through the Declaration
of Independence, held that all men are created

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:31.880
equal. They're endowed by their creator with
certain unalienable rights, that among those

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.840
are life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.
Let's try to figure out what those things

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.130
meant. Yes?

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:44.070
In the USA we were founded on universal principles,
and the answer key, if you will, for those

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.550
principles, is the Declaration of Independence.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.530
The explicit purpose of government is to preserve
those rights from any intrusions of government

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:51.740
on that individual.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.280
Yes, ma'am?

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.570
That's what the role of government does. It
doesn't give us a right. We have our natural,

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:02.860
inherent rights, from God, and the government
is there to protect those for us.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:09.060
I think the most important word in the Declaration
is "secure." Governments are instituted among

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:17.260
men to secure their rights, not give us rights.
The rights pre-exist government.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:24.020
Inspired by the ideals of America's founding,
a small group of ministers started a college

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.990
out on what was then the frontier.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:31.100
Hillsdale was founded in 1844 by a bunch of
New England preachers moving out on the edge

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:38.830
of civilization. They started it with next
to nothing. One of our founders went riding

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:45.270
his horse for two years around Michigan and
Indiana and Illinois and Ohio and Wisconsin.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:48.790
We still have the list of the people who gave
three and five dollars at a time. Came back

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:54.250
with twenty-five thousand dollars, a princely
sum.

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:58.040
From the beginning they were guided by the
principles of the Declaration that all would

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.040
be welcome in the pursuit of the life of the
mind, regardless of gender and regardless

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.630
of race.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:09.230
We think it may be the first institution in
history, anywhere, any time, any kind of institution,

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:16.769
with a charter that says, "We'll take our
student body without regard to race, sex or

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:18.769
national origin."

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:23.610
That's what civil rights leaders of the 60s
aimed for. If you said to Dr. King, "Fifty

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:29.449
years hence, what would you like the society
to be like?" I suspect he could look at Hillsdale

00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:43.759
and say, "I'll take that."

00:04:43.759 --> 00:04:49.330
Being lovers of freedom, the founders of Hillsdale
were deeply involved in the slavery crisis

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:55.069
and had a lot to do with the founding of the
party that elected Abraham Lincoln president.

00:04:55.069 --> 00:04:59.569
The platform on which Abraham Lincoln was
elected President of the United States was

00:04:59.569 --> 00:05:04.400
written, in the main, by two Hillsdale College
professors.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.960
Lincoln said in the Gettysburg Address, "Four
score and seven years ago, our fathers brought

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.110
forth on this continent a new nation, conceived
in liberty and dedicated to the proposition

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:19.219
that all men are created equal." Where would
he have taken his instruction from ideas of

00:05:19.219 --> 00:05:24.370
liberty? Yes, sir, Mr. Brooks?

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:28.569
He would have gotten the idea of liberty and
the United States being conceived in liberty

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:30.379
from the Declaration of Independence.

00:05:30.379 --> 00:05:36.680
At the time, there were people arguing that
your rights came from things like your race

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:42.550
or your status in society, but Lincoln's argument
was that it came from your status as a human

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:43.270
being.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:44.729
Yes, sir?

00:05:44.729 --> 00:05:51.219
During the revolution, we claimed that all
men were created equal. In the Civil War,

00:05:51.219 --> 00:05:59.789
we fought to prove that.

00:05:59.789 --> 00:06:04.309
Walking the campus with Dr. Arnn, he stops
in front of the monument, and he says, "When

00:06:04.309 --> 00:06:11.770
the Civil War broke out, every male student
left Hillsdale to enlist."

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:17.309
The Battle of Gettysburg would have gone differently
if there hadn't been about thirty or forty

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:22.419
boys from Hillsdale College in the worst place
in the battlefield. By the way, we didn't

00:06:22.419 --> 00:06:34.759
make those boys go. We taught them to love
freedom, and they rallied to its defense.

00:06:34.759 --> 00:06:40.330
The next major challenge to these principles,
the principles of the founding, emerged in

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:43.819
the late nineteenth century: Progressivism.

00:06:43.819 --> 00:06:50.559
Woodrow Wilson, elected president in 1912,
became the first President to criticize the

00:06:50.559 --> 00:06:56.009
American founding, which he did root and branch.
It was not a peripheral criticism. He said

00:06:56.009 --> 00:07:03.839
the Founders may have been right in their
simple era, but we complex, modern people

00:07:03.839 --> 00:07:09.710
with our complex, modern nation, know that
limited government's a mistake. Therefore,

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:17.819
the separation of powers is a mistake. Therefore,
the idea that government exists solely to

00:07:17.819 --> 00:07:22.930
protect pre-existing rights is not nearly
ambitious enough.

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:27.869
He thought that his political science was
far in advance of the Founders and that we

00:07:27.869 --> 00:07:37.289
needed a kind of re-founding of the country's
politics in order to stay abreast of the times.

00:07:37.289 --> 00:07:42.180
I think Woodrow Wilson's thoughts make sense
at first glance, because he was saying, "How

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:47.339
can two documents from the seventeen hundreds
govern us in an age where now we have airplanes,

00:07:47.339 --> 00:07:53.169
we have cars ..." I think it makes sense in
that regard. But when you think about the

00:07:53.169 --> 00:07:55.539
fact that human nature has not changed ...

00:07:55.539 --> 00:08:01.580
What Wilson is saying is essentially the exact
opposite, that things need to adapt and change

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:05.099
over time. In a sense, that they are not eternal.

00:08:05.099 --> 00:08:09.020
Progressives believe that there are superior
intellects and that superior intellects ought

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:14.770
to help and sometimes coerce their fellow
human beings into a better state of perfection.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:20.740
They're all about changing you and me, and
they are not about unchanging truths and natural

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:25.229
rights. They're about the perfectibility of
the human species. Very dangerous to believe

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:26.699
in that, and they do.

00:08:26.699 --> 00:08:34.339
The Wilsonian tradition went from there to
today, where it's enthroned, as it were, in

00:08:34.339 --> 00:08:43.150
the halls of Congress and in power. The Progressives
went from Woodrow Wilson to the presidency

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:50.260
of his former Assistant Secretary of the Navy,
Franklin Roosevelt, to Franklin Roosevelt's

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:57.280
young protégé from Texas, young Congressman
Lyndon Johnson, next to be president, to the

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:04.070
fourth transformative Progressive president,
Barack Obama.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:11.580
As government continued to expand, inevitably
it intruded into higher education. Successive

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:19.140
administrations issued ever more onerous regulations,
forcing colleges to comply if they took Federal

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:27.010
money, which Hillsdale never had. Then the
government tightened the noose.

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:32.430
In the Carter administration, we heard from
the Department of Health, Education and Welfare.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:36.960
They said, "We realize you don't take any
direct aid to the college from the Federal

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:40.740
government, but your students are getting
aid like the G.I. Bill. Because of that, we

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:45.810
get to regulate you comprehensively. Whatever
we want. Hundreds of pages of stuff. You have

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:53.090
to comply." We sued them, and the suit went
all the way to the Supreme Court and we lost.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:59.770
To be free from government intrusion, we have
refused all Federal funding, direct or indirect.

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:06.650
The heavy hand of Liberalism would have been
brought to bear on education at Hillsdale,

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:11.770
both in terms of the content of the student
body, but eventually in terms of who was hired

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:19.220
for the faculty, what is being taught in the
classroom ... There really is a ... You would

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:25.720
be making a bargain with the devil if you
had conceded an inch to them on this question,

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:28.660
and to Hillsdale's credit, it did not concede
an inch.

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:34.180
A college is an expensive thing to run, and
most of our competitors are getting thirty

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:40.780
percent of what they call student revenues
from the government, and so we make that up.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:45.760
We make that up by hundreds of thousands of
people have been generous with our college.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:51.060
My mother died, left me a fortune. What better
way than to help people go to Hillsdale?

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:52.640
Wow, that's incredible.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:58.020
Unlike the distant Federal government, Hillsdale's
donors know the students they support.

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:02.590
I'm working my way through college and I'm
just really, really grateful My parents, every

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:04.660
day, are so grateful as well.

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:07.690
I appreciate the letters I get from you all.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:08.390
Good, good.

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:10.140
I really enjoy them. Highlight of my week.

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:11.390
Oh, good! I'm so glad.

00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:20.510
Each one of these bubbles is full of the gas
methane.

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:27.160
Yeah!

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:35.810
With its hard-won independence, Hillsdale
is free to pursue truth and defend liberty.

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:43.200
Where are we going to go from here? What do
we want the college to be like in ten years?

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:48.560
The center of gravity will have shifted. What
is now an exciting on-campus phenomenon will

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:52.070
be recognized nationwide.

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:58.240
Because of its unique mission, Hillsdale has
drawn major international leaders to its campus

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:05.260
and events. The best of these speeches are
put into a publication called Imprimis, which

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:07.880
reaches millions of people nationwide.

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:12.170
I heard of it through a friend of mine who
I think was the first conservative I ever

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:15.110
met, and so he gave me a copy of Imprimis.

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:20.090
I need to make a switch on the October Imprimis.

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:26.420
My journey to a new political understanding
took a great deal of time and a certain amount

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:34.070
of salubrious pain. I was impressed, first
of all, by the exactitude of the writing in

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:38.470
Imprimis. It was academic in the best sense,
sort of tempted back to take one back to first

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:41.260
principles and to state them cogently.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:41.730
Hi!

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:46.040
Good morning, Miss Louis. Could you tell me
a bit about your graduate school of statesmanship?

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:46.450
Here's a brochure-

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:52.920
This is the first graduate school at Hillsdale.
Its M.A.s and Ph.D.s in politics will spread

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:57.990
Hillsdale's learning to universities and other
institutions across the nation.

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:03.810
You'll be focusing mostly on the first principles
of American Constitutionalism and the fate

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:05.990
of those principles over time.

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:11.560
It gives a degree in the great books that
are necessary to understand the American Revolution

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:13.510
and politics and man in general.

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:16.950
They keep saying all we need is more regulation.
It seems like government's always the solution-

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:22.410
The Kirby Center in Washington, D.C., brings
Hillsdale students to the Capitol for classes

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:28.060
and internships. It also offers educational
programs for Washington policy-makers.

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:34.110
It's in the belly of the beast, speaking truth
to power. It's a kind of reverse-missionary

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:39.760
work in which, from the provinces you send
missionaries to the center, rather than the

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:44.300
other way around, and that's because they're
most needed in the center.

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:49.710
Hello and welcome to Constitution 201, Hillsdale's
online course on the Constitution and the

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:50.160
Progressive. My name's-

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:54.500
Hillsdale's free online classes for citizens
nationwide who want to learn.

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:58.940
What evidence do you have that it's possible
to recover the older understanding of the

00:13:58.940 --> 00:13:59.590
Constitution?

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:03.560
Historical parallel would be Lincoln, who
did just this. He showed that it was possible-

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:08.510
Your first Constitution course, with two hundred
thousand people signed up to listen to the

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:13.780
great scholars of Hillsdale really, I think,
surprised me and surprised everyone else that

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:19.470
there was that kind of hunger for that kind
of serious but very winsome learning.

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:26.690
Please join us next week.

00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:36.960
In every major crisis since the middle of
the nineteenth century, including the Civil

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:44.330
War, our college has played an important part,
and always the same part. We are faithful

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:49.440
to the things we were founded to do, and they
are just like the things that the country

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:51.080
was founded to do.

00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:56.670
It sounds odd to say that this little college
in this little corner of southeastern Michigan

00:14:56.670 --> 00:15:01.570
can lead a renewal, but it can.

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:06.990
One thing that has made me the most excited
about learning, I think, is looking at history

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:10.680
and seeing the ideas that have shaped our
own country, and that is what our purpose

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:14.930
is here, to become men and women of character
who make a difference in this country.

00:15:14.930 --> 00:15:21.730
That's the lesson of Hillsdale. Let's create
a more just society. What guides do we have?

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:27.310
Oh, I get it. Let's go back to the Constitution.
Let's share this basic texts.

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:31.570
The threads that run through these books will
run through my own life and beyond; when I'm

00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:36.410
not here anymore there's something of value
that continues on. I think that's what's so

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:38.740
beautiful about it.

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:44.060
Hillsdale is producing a generation of missionaries
for liberty, and that's a very good thing

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:46.880
for a world that's been long-starved for that.

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:57.150
We do it to produce a small leavening minority
that will radiate through the whole republic.

00:15:57.150 --> 00:16:03.320
We can take that Greco-Roman and Judeo-Christian
heritage and see how America was founded upon

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:09.740
those ideals, and then we are stewards of
that education today. As we leave Hillsdale,

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:16.600
we take with us these ideas and we rise to
self-government through education.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:24.160
The college has ever been faithful to its
founding principles, which are sound learning,

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:29.850
knowing the beautiful and the good and the
true things, in support of civil and religious

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:45.190
freedom and intelligent piety. That's what
the college is for. It's still for that.

